    <div>
      <span class="win"><span>Month</span><span>End</span></span>
      <h2>The five months that need your help</h2>
      <p>A simple date watch counts to 31 every month. That means it needs a quick correction after every shorter month.</p>
      <ul class="dl"><li>February, after the 28th, or the 29th in a leap year</li><li>April, after the 30th</li><li>June, after the 30th</li><li>September, after the 30th</li><li>November, after the 30th</li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap faq"><span class="win"><span>FAQ</span><span>Answers</span></span><h2 style="margin-bottom:20px">Calendar watch FAQ</h2><details open><summary>What is a day-date watch?</summary><p>A day-date watch shows both the day of the week and the date of the month, usually in small windows on the dial. The day often appears at 12 o&#8217;clock and the date at 3 o&#8217;clock, though layouts vary.</p></details><details><summary>Why does my watch show the 31st in a 30-day month?</summary><p>Most simple date mechanisms have 31 positions and can&#8217;t tell how long a month is. After a 30-day month, or February, you need to advance the date by hand.</p></details><details><summary>When is it unsafe to change the date?</summary><p>On many mechanical watches, the date-change gears engage roughly between 9 pm and 3 am. Using the quick-set during that window can strain or damage the mechanism, so move the hands outside it first.</p></details><details><summary>What&#8217;s the difference between annual and perpetual calendars?</summary><p>An annual calendar knows the length of 30- and 31-day months and needs correcting once a year, at the end of February. A perpetual calendar also tracks February and leap years, usually needing no correction until 2100.</p></details><details><summary>How accurate is a moon phase display?</summary><p>A typical moon phase gear drifts by about a day every two to three years. More advanced designs can stay accurate for over a century before needing adjustment.</p></details></div></section>

